[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. Composition is centered, shot from a 3/4 overhead angle (40-degree) to capture the volume of the stuffed dates while maintaining generous negative space around the edges. Shallow depth of field keeps the front date in sharp focus with the background softly blurred. Lighting is soft, natural side light from a window on the left, casting subtle, gentle shadows across the surface. Setting features a weathered, warm-toned dark oak wooden board with subtle wood grain. A matte ceramic plate holds the dish. Props include a small, rustic ramekin containing a smear of leftover almond-cocoa paste in the background and a few stray, irregularly chopped walnut pieces scattered naturally near the plate. The dish consists of two large, succulent, dark amber Medjool dates, halved lengthwise and laid flat, cut-side up. The cavities are generously filled with a rich, velvety dark-chocolate-colored almond butter paste. Each date is topped with unevenly scattered, crunchy brown walnut chunks pressed slightly into the filling. A light, delicate dusting of white flaky sea salt crystals glitters on the surface. The presentation is casual and homemade, not stylized. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish placement, a small smudge of cocoa-almond butter on the plate rim. Subtle traces of the process include a faint crumb trail on the wood. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature — cozy and inviting. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. Indistinguishable from professional food photography."
]
